    "reply": "The knowledge graph returns two DNA mismatch repair genes for this question. The first is NCBIGene:4292, mutL homolog 1 [1]. The second is NCBIGene:4436, mutS homolog 2 [5].\n\n## Linked hereditary syndromes\n\nLynch syndrome 1 is among the retrieved conditions [6]. Colorectal cancer, hereditary nonpolyposis, type 2 is another [3]. Muir-Torr\u00c3\u00a9 syndrome is also retrieved [2]. Mismatch repair cancer syndrome 1 appears as well [4]. Mismatch repair cancer syndrome 2 is the last retrieved condition [7].\n\n## Mechanistic role\n\nThe RefSeq summary for the mutL gene describes a mismatch repair heterodimer [17: \"can heterodimerize with mismatch repair endonuclease PMS2 to form MutL alpha, part of the DNA mismatch repair system\"]. It describes how this complex works with MutS proteins to start excision near mismatches [17: \"When MutL alpha is bound by MutS beta and some accessory proteins, the PMS2 subunit of MutL alpha introduces a single-strand break near DNA mismatches\"]. The same summary links the gene directly to colorectal cancer predisposition [17: \"This gene was identified as a locus frequently mutated in hereditary nonpolyposis colon cancer (HNPCC)\"].\n\n## Variant interpretation and trials\n\nA single functional assay has been used to classify uncertain variants in both genes [24: \"Functional Assay to Assess Variants of Unknown Significance in the MLH1 and MSH2 Genes and Identify Patients With Lynch Syndrome\"]. Clinical trials in this area include immunotherapy for mismatch-repair-deficient tumours [18: \"Study of MK-3475 in Patients With Microsatellite Unstable (MSI) Tumors\"].",
